        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "4b78cc34-a749-5131-939b-5bd85145e3d1",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} + 6 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 3\\right)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} + 6 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 3\\right)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & - 2 v - 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 2)\\cdot (- 2 v - 6)-(2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 12 u - 4 v - 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 12 u - 4 v - 6 &<0 \\\\ \\left(u + 1\\right) \\left(v + 3\\right) > \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v > -3 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}$ for $u>-1$ and $v < -3 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}$ for $u<-1.$ At $u=-1,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line is excluded from the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -3 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186129283/plot-4ffed708e8033c8201bf.png)",
